<Article slug="clients-and-latency">
<p>A product page needs six cached fragments: the product card, seller summary, delivery estimate, recommendations, review summary and category navigation. Each key is already known. Each answer is in Redis.</p>
<p>The application sends one <code>GET</code>, waits for its reply, then sends the next. Redis spends little time executing the commands, but the page waits for six trips across the network. A cache hit has avoided source work without necessarily avoiding much waiting.</p>
<p>In the illustration, each round trip takes 20 ms and each command takes 2 ms to execute. Play the sequential requests, then change the batch size to six and replay.</p>
<RedisPipeline />
<p>The sequential version receives its final reply at 132 ms: six repetitions of 20 ms travel plus 2 ms execution. Sending all six commands without waiting between them brings the last reply forward to 32 ms. The server still performs twelve milliseconds of work, one command after another. The difference is the serial waiting around that work.</p>

<h2>Pipelining removes serial round-trip waits</h2>
<p><strong>Pipelining</strong> means issuing multiple commands without waiting for each preceding reply. It lets network travel and server work overlap across the commands. Replies still have to reach the client, and the client must associate each reply with the corresponding operation.</p>
<p>The animation deliberately separates propagation, server queueing and execution. With one pipeline, all six tiny requests reach the model server together. The first can execute immediately; the others wait their turns. Their return journeys overlap. Pipelining has not created six execution lanes inside Redis.</p>
<p>Increase the round-trip time. The gap between the two approaches grows because the sequential path keeps paying that delay before it can make progress. Reduce it and the advantage shrinks, while the twelve milliseconds of execution stay fixed. The example omits payload transmission and other clients; it is a model of dependency on replies, not a Redis benchmark.</p>
<p>There can also be efficiency gains from handling several commands in fewer socket operations. Neither effect implies that an arbitrarily large pipeline is desirable. The useful question is how much independent work can be kept in flight within the latency, memory and fairness budgets.</p>
<p>The word <em>independent</em> matters. If the application must read a value, compute a new value from it and then write the result, it cannot construct that write until the read completes. A pipeline cannot remove a dependency in the application’s logic. A suitable Redis command or server-side script may move that computation to the server; the next article develops the atomicity requirements.</p>
<p>For several string keys, <code>MGET</code> can express a multi-key read directly. That is one Redis operation, while a pipeline contains several operations. Neither choice removes response bytes or makes unrelated application data automatically consistent. Redis Cluster introduces key-slot constraints for multi-key operations; we will follow those in the Cluster article.</p>

<h2>Pipelining with node-redis</h2>
<p>Client libraries differ in how batching is exposed. In node-redis 6.3.0, which the accompanying lab pins, an explicit pipeline can be built as follows:</p>
<pre><code>{`const replies = await client.multi()
  .get('product:42')
  .get('seller:7')
  .get('reviews:42')
  .execAsPipeline();`}</code></pre>
<p>The method name <code>multi()</code> is easy to misread. Here, <code>execAsPipeline()</code> sends a pipeline. Using <code>exec()</code> instead requests a Redis transaction. Decide which semantics you need rather than treating the two endings as performance alternatives.</p>
<p>node-redis also automatically pipelines commands issued in the same event-loop tick. Starting independent operations together, for example in a bounded <code>Promise.all</code>, can use this behaviour. An <code>await</code> after every <code>GET</code> makes later calls depend on earlier replies and defeats that opportunity.</p>
<p>“Start together” must not become “start everything”. Mapping an enormous key list into promises can allocate a large amount of queued work before Redis has answered any of it. Bound the batch and the total work in flight. Measure the useful operation, such as rendering a product page, as well as individual command throughput.</p>
<p>A pipeline is not an all-or-nothing unit. The lab sends a valid <code>SET</code>, an invalid integer increment and another valid <code>SET</code>. The pipeline promise rejects, yet an independent read confirms that the final write succeeded. A single rejected aggregate result does not establish that none of its commands ran.</p>
<p>Check how your chosen client reports per-command errors and handles a disconnection partway through a batch. Commands still queued locally and commands already written to a connection can have different fates. Retrying the whole batch blindly can repeat successful work. Transaction and retry decisions need operation-specific reasoning.</p>

<h2>Batch size and head-of-line blocking</h2>
<p>Six small replies and six multi-megabyte replies have the same command count but very different costs. Redis, the operating system and the client may all hold buffered data. The application may then allocate more memory while decoding or deserialising those replies.</p>
<p>On an ordinary command connection, reply bytes form an ordered stream. A small reply behind a large one cannot simply jump ahead. This is a form of <strong>head-of-line blocking</strong>: later work waits behind earlier work sharing an ordered resource.</p>
<p>Compare a large reply with an expensive command below. Then give the small request another connection.</p>
<ResponseQueue />
<p>For the large reply, a separate stream removes the requirement that the small reply’s bytes follow it. The model gives each stream its own transmission budget to isolate that ordering effect. A real shared network link can still impose contention, so the displayed improvement is not a prediction for your network.</p>
<p>For expensive execution, a second connection does not remove the shared server work ahead of the small command. In this model, both commands use the same execution lane. The small request waits for that lane regardless of which connection will carry its reply.</p>
<p>This distinction helps choose a remedy. Reducing a response’s size addresses byte transfer, buffering and parsing. Replacing an expensive command addresses execution. Isolating latency-sensitive requests from bulk traffic can address client or connection contention. Adding connections without identifying the shared resource may only add more queued demand.</p>
<p>A batch limit should therefore account for estimated request and response bytes, command cost and acceptable waiting time, as well as the number of commands. If batching waits to collect more work, that collection delay belongs in the request’s latency budget too. Sending a partially filled batch after a short bound can matter more than achieving a preferred batch size.</p>

<h2>Connection reuse, pooling and multiplexing</h2>
<p>Creating a connection for every cache operation adds work before the first useful command: establishing the connection and, where configured, authentication and TLS negotiation. Reuse established connections through the client’s supported lifecycle, rather than hiding connection creation inside each cache lookup.</p>
<p>A <strong>connection pool</strong> lends existing connections to operations and returns them for reuse. A <strong>multiplexer</strong> lets multiple callers share a connection, matching replies back to their operations. Libraries can support one approach, the other, or a combination. Inspect the library rather than assuming that “the Redis client” means a single universal design.</p>
<p>Both approaches need bounds. A pool with every connection busy needs a policy for waiting or rejection, not unlimited growth. A shared connection needs a bound on pending commands and bytes. Pool-acquisition time can be substantial even when Redis executes each command quickly.</p>
<p>Connection-specific operations need special care. A blocking list read can hold up other work on the same connection. <code>WATCH</code> creates state associated with a connection, so unrelated callers must not accidentally share that transaction context. Use the library’s supported isolated connection or pool mechanism for such work.</p>
<p>More connections do not create more Redis execution capacity automatically. They can separate client queues or blocking operations, but they also consume resources and can expand aggregate concurrency. Choose the smallest arrangement that meets the measured workload and isolation needs, then test it under contention.</p>

<h2>Client queues, timeouts and cancellation</h2>
<p>A cache operation passes through several states: waiting in the client, written to the connection, executed by Redis, and answered to the application. A timeout or cancellation mechanism may cover only some of those states.</p>
<p>The distinction is concrete in node-redis 6.3.0. Inspection of its published implementation shows that command timeout and abort listeners are removed when a command leaves the unsent queue. In the lab, a command configured with a 40 ms timeout is sent while Redis processing is deliberately paused. After 120 ms its promise is still pending; later it resolves successfully and its write is visible.</p>
<p>That observation is specific to the pinned version. It is why a configuration property named <code>timeout</code> should not be treated as a complete end-to-end deadline without checking what it controls.</p>
<ClientLifecycle />
<p>Expire the queue timer before sending: this unsent attempt is removed and rejected. Reset, send the command and end the application’s wait instead. Redis can still execute it and return a late success. The application deadline in this model abandons waiting without cancelling the underlying operation.</p>
<p>Now compare losing the connection before and after execution. The displayed server state differs, but the application obtains the same connection error. The illustration gives you an observer’s view that the caller does not have. From the missing reply alone, the caller cannot establish whether the write happened.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/redis-client-lab.zip" download>downloadable Redis client lab</a> verifies these boundaries with node-redis 6.3.0 and Redis 8.2.2. Unzip it, run <code>npm ci --ignore-scripts</code>, then run <code>node run.mjs</code> with the pinned Redis binary on your path, or set <code>REDIS_SERVER</code> to its absolute path. The README includes the tested build command.</p>
<p>The runner starts its own temporary Redis process on a private Unix socket, with persistence disabled, and cleans it up afterwards. Two readiness tests use a controlled local TCP peer that accepts a connection but never answers the handshake; it executes no Redis commands. With offline queuing enabled, an unsent <code>GET</code> expires in the client. With it disabled, the operation rejects immediately. Neither request reaches that peer.</p>
<p>A further test pauses Redis and fills a configured queue limit with eight commands. A ninth is refused, while the earlier operations later complete. The small bound is an experiment, not a recommended setting. Connection-handshake commands also need room, so an extremely small limit can prevent the client becoming ready at all.</p>
<p>Keep separate budgets for connection establishment, queue or pool waiting, socket inactivity and the application’s overall request deadline. They address different waits. If the application stops awaiting a command, continue accounting for any underlying work or buffers that remain. Destroying a shared connection can affect many callers and still does not revoke a write Redis has already executed.</p>

<h2>Client latency and Redis SLOWLOG</h2>
<p>Fast Redis command execution and a slow application request can both be true. Before a command reaches Redis, it may wait for a pool slot, an event-loop turn, a batch flush or a reconnect. Afterwards, it may wait behind reply bytes, parsing, deserialisation and application scheduling.</p>
<p>Redis <code>SLOWLOG</code> measures command execution, excluding network I/O and reply transfer. It is useful evidence about one part of the path. An empty slow log does not prove that cache calls are fast from the application’s perspective.</p>
<p>Measure the complete cache-call duration in the application, including client waiting, and use client diagnostics to separate queueing or connection events where available. Compare that with server execution evidence and representative network measurements. A tiny <code>PING</code> on an otherwise idle connection is not a substitute for the real workload’s response sizes and concurrency.</p>
<p>Record distributions and tails, not only averages. A short run with warm connections, small values and no competing work can hide the condition that matters: a large reply, a full client queue, connection recovery or an expensive neighbouring command. Keep the workload, topology and client version alongside any performance claim.</p>
<p>Return to the opening page. First remove unnecessary serial dependencies between its independent reads. Then bound the work and bytes in flight, choose connection isolation deliberately, and enforce a request deadline with the actual client semantics in mind. Batching can make the page faster; it does not make its reads and writes atomic. That is the subject of <a href="/learn/databases/caching/atomicity/">Redis transactions and Lua scripts</a>.</p>

